  "result": "Fixed. The bug was a one-character mistake in `Store.save_order` (`shop/storage.py:17`):\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] == order.id]   # before\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] != order.id]   # after\n```\n\n`save_order` is meant to replace any existing row for the order and keep all the others. The inverted comparison did the opposite: it kept only the row with the matching id and threw away every other order. Saving order A and then order B left only B in the file. With `!=`, saving a new order adds it, and saving an existing one replaces its row.\n\n**Tests:** All 8 pass. No tests covered `Store` before, so I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two:\n- saving two different orders keeps both;\n- saving an order again after changing its state replaces its row without dropping the other order.\n\nI also ran the new tests against the old code, and both failed, so they do catch this bug.\n\nThe changes are uncommitted: the edit to `shop/storage.py` and the new test file.\n\nToken Beggar: routed 0 subtasks (debugging stays inline on claude-opus-5-5)",
